    "comment_id": 4439271457,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/index.ts",
    "line": 770,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Scratch parts swept on a launch where detection missed are placed with link(), possibly into a synced folder**\n\nThe sweep now reads the scratch folder whatever detection says (line 765), and the comment in `stranded-recordings.ts:26-28` names the case it is for: a launch where detection did not answer. But `placeByRename` still comes from `syncedStagingFor(vaultDir) !== null`. On that same launch it is false, so `placeTake` (`recording-writer.ts:113`) uses `placeNew`, which hard-links the scratch part into the workspace folder. A Google Drive folder outside `~/Library/CloudStorage` is recognised only by its xattr, and the path rule in `SyncStates.of` (`sync-state.ts:29`) misses it. If that xattr probe times out, the newly swept scratch take is linked into Drive, which is the hazard decision 15 and NIW2-577 forbid. A part in the scratch folder only exists because the workspace was synced when it was recorded, so you could always place parts from `extraStagingDirs` by rename. `placeByRename` already copies on EXDEV (`safe-place.ts:320-321`), so this is also safe for a vault on another volume.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 77d67e59. As you suggest, a take found in an `extraStagingDirs` folder is always placed by rename, whatever `placeByRename` says: a part there exists only because its workspace was synced when it was recorded. `placeByRename` already copies on EXDEV. New test: a scratch take swept with `placeByRename: false` is recovered with zero `link()` calls."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439271462,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/cloud/workspace-cloud-section.tsx",
    "line": 42,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A failed read after a workspace switch keeps showing the previous workspace's state**\n\n`loadFailed` only renders while `state` is null (line 80). After a switch, `workspaces.epoch` changes and `load` runs again. If that read fails (for example, maintenance refuses `workspacesCloudState`), `setLoadFailed(true)` has no visible effect. The section keeps the previous workspace's location, filter and unassigned list under the new workspace's name (`active` comes from `workspaces.active`, line 51), which is the mismatch the request counter was added to prevent. You could clear `state` on failure (`setState(null)` next to `setLoadFailed(true)`), or clear it when the epoch changes.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 77d67e59. A failed read now clears `state` as well as setting the error, so after a switch the section shows the alert instead of the previous workspace's location, filter and list. New test: a good read, then a broadcast whose read fails. The alert shows and the location row is gone."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439296845,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/library/dataless.ts",
    "line": 79,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡📝 **Consider · docs: The deadline is checked only between batches, so a probe can run past 10 s**\n\n`now() >= deadline` is checked only before a batch starts (line 79). A batch that starts at 9.9 s can still take `STAT_TIMEOUT_MS` (5 s, line 19). If `stat` is killed by that timeout and prints nothing, the `lstat` fallback (lines 94-97) then runs over up to 200 paths with no time limit, and that is exactly the stalled-provider case. So the real bound is about deadline + 5 s, plus whatever the fallback takes.\n\nTwo places promise a hard bound: line 59 (\"The longest a whole probe may take\") and the spec (`2026-10-29-several-workspaces-design.md:1328-1329`, \"bounded at 10 s for the whole listing\"). Either reword both to say the deadline is checked per batch (worst case about 15 s), or check `now()` inside the fallback loop and treat the paths left over as evicted.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 4ed15804, with the second option (no rewording needed): `now()` is checked inside the fallback loop, and every `stat`/`lstat` wait is cut at what is left of the deadline, so 10 s is a real bound. The code comment and the spec now say so (\"a hard 10 s bound for the whole listing: every `stat` call and every fallback `lstat` waits at most for what is left of it\"). Tests cover a hanging `stat` and a stalled `lstat`."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439296846,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/library/dataless.test.ts",
    "line": 134,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧪 **Consider · tests: `now = 5_000` comes after the last call, so the TTL expiry is never tested**\n\nThe test name says an evicted answer is remembered \"for the window\", and it does check that the answer is reused inside the window. But `now = 5_000` runs after the last `isEvicted` call, so the clock change does nothing. Nothing checks that an evicted answer older than `ttlMs` gets probed again (`dataless.ts:127`). Move the clock change before another `isEvicted(\"/x\")` call, without calling `forget()` first, and expect the probe count to go up. As written, the line looks like a check but tests nothing.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 4ed15804. The clock now moves past the TTL **before** another `isEvicted(\"/x\")`, without `forget()`, and the probe count goes from 1 to 2. Then `forget()` within the TTL makes the next read ask at once (count 3). The test is renamed \"remembers an evicted answer for the TTL only…\"."
      }
    ]
  }
]

